    "turn_idx": 79,
    "turn_text": "Well, Buck, you hit the nail on the head. I think this will be one of Chuck Schumer. Hopefully, he's only a point of pride in bringing in the most radical appointees to the federal bench that we've ever seen. The way it works in the Senate for what they call executive-caleter appointments is that rather than a 60-vote threshold, which is what we have for legislation, there's only a 50-plus one threshold for these appointees. Schumer has the votes. And so we push back hard at every turn. They put some absolutely terrible nominees forward. People without relevant experience. People that have no business in the courtroom, they don't have any, you know, any capacity. They couldn't be in front of a judge. Why in the world are they going to be behind the, behind the bench? But that's where they're moving because the credentials they're looking for are radical credentials. or they're just looking for some type of demographic credential that they can pack there, that they can pat themselves in the back about accomplishing, but but confidence seems to be the lowest tick mark on that list book. Confident seems to be the last thing considered. It's everything else from from ideology to demographics that seem to be driving that process. Chuck Schumer learned his lesson from Mitch McConnell. If you go back to the previous Trump administration, President Trump and Leader McConnell worked on putting a lot of strong conservative judges into the judiciary. And Chuck Schumer learned from that playbook. They're doing the same thing. I think it will continue to be a key element of strategy depending on which party controls the Senate. They will work. They'll be focused on that very much.",
